course: dictionary
lesson: 5679
title: Loanwords
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A loanword is a word that one language has taken from another and now uses as its own. «Pizza» came into English from Italian, and we still say it almost the same way.
explain: Loanwords often keep spellings that look odd in English, and that is a clue to where they came from. «Ballet» and «café» come from French, so their accents and silent endings look French.
explain: Some borrowed words are chosen for their sound. «kělè» («KUH-luh») is cola, a loanword; the kě sounds like the word for thirsty, which makes it a fitting name.
fact: A loanword is a word one language has taken from another and now uses as its own.
fact: «Pizza» is a loanword from Italian, and «kindergarten» is a loanword from German.
fact: «Tsunami» is a loanword from Japanese, and «kangaroo» comes from an Australian Aboriginal language, Guugu Yimithirr.
fact: English is full of loanwords from French, Latin, Norse, Greek and many more.
fact: Knowing that a word is a loanword helps you guess how it sounds and why it is spelt the way it is.
exercise: «Ballet» and «café» are loanwords from which language? | *French | German | Japanese | Italian
exercise: «Tsunami» is a loanword from which language? | Norse | Latin | Greek | *Japanese
exercise: What is a loanword? | Any word that is very long | *A word one language has taken from another and now uses as its own | A word that is only used in poems | A word with no meaning
test: Why might «ballet» look odd in English? | It has no vowels | It is a very old English word | *It was borrowed from French and keeps some French spelling | It was made up by a poet
test: Which word is a loanword from German? | Tsunami | Kangaroo | Café | *Kindergarten
exam: Ella hears the word «pizza» and wonders where it comes from. What is the most likely answer? | *It is a loanword from Italian | It was invented by Ella | It is a loanword from Guugu Yimithirr | It is a loanword from Japanese
